I'm Prannoy. My first job was teaching pre-algebra to 7th graders in Baltimore through Teach for America, with an education degree from Johns Hopkins earned on nights and weekends. I have not really changed jobs since. I founded Bansho, an ed-tech company that began as a short-form video app for teachers, grew into a tutoring marketplace at close to $1M in annual recurring revenue, and sold two years in. I spent more than four years at Audible shipping AI features. Now I lead product on an AI tutor, and I still build the prototype when that is faster than describing it.

Twelve-year-olds do not hand over their attention because a title says teacher. A teacher earns it in the first two weeks or spends the rest of the year chasing it. Thirty of them move in the same direction only when the work in front of them beats whatever is in their pockets.

Everything since has been that problem with a bigger budget. A learning product promises that someone will be better off at the end than they were at the start, and most of the industry cannot say whether it delivered. The arc I care about runs all the way through: someone learns something, proves they can do it, and gets paid for it.

I also write poems. They have appeared in Delta Poetry Review, Wildscape Literary Journal, and Indelible Literary and Arts Journal, and a first collection is coming together slowly. I hold them to the same standard as the products. A stranger spends time on this, and something should be different at the end.
